Hi Finn,

Thank you so much this explanation. It really helps! So if I am understanding this correctly, the covariates (continuous or categorial) need to be provided as some sort of gridded object like a SpatialPixelsDataFrame, raster layer, etc. and not extracting the covariate at the locations before hand (for example using the f.covar function and then creating the model like so.....

ecomp <- coordinates ~ elev +  mySmooth(coordinates, model = matern) + Intercept(1)
...where elev is the covariate values at each location of what you are studying).

What if, lets say, the locations for what you are trying to model are matched with monthly or seasonal environmental data (i.e. not static like elevation). I assume then this framework, where we are using a single gridded object, would not work and I would need to construct the integration scheme for the temporal scale at which the locations are at (i.e. construct an integration scheme at the month/seasonal scale and match them with the month / seasonal environmental data). This seems like I would need to construct a spatiotemporal LGCP model following the methods here <https://inlabru-org.github.io/inlabru/articles/2d_lgcp_spatiotemporal.html>. However, how would I make sure that the integration points for each month or season is matched with the concurrent environmental data?

Hopefully this all makes sense! Again, thanks for your help!

-Nima

